Why is clone() outdated?

A few real problems:

Shallow copy by default — Object.clone() copies fields byte-for-byte. If your class has a reference field (like a List), the clone shares the same underlying object — mutate one, you mutate both.
Bypasses constructors — clone() doesn't call your class's constructor at all. Any validation/setup logic you wrote there never runs on the cloned object.
Awkward with final fields — clone() can't easily let you reassign a final field to a proper deep copy during cloning.
Cloneable is a broken marker interface — it has no methods. It doesn't declare clone(). It just flips a hidden flag; calling clone() on a class that forgot to implement Cloneable throws CloneNotSupportedException — a checked exception, forcing ugly try/catch everywhere.

Modern replacement: copy constructors or static factory methods —

```java
public Person(Person other) {
    this.name = other.name;
    this.age = other.age;
}
```

Clear, uses the constructor properly, and you control exactly what's deep-copied vs shared.